---
/**
 * Chunk Visualizer tool page.
 */
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ChunkVisualizer from '../../components/tools/ChunkVisualizer';
---

<ToolLayout slug="chunk-visualizer">
  <ChunkVisualizer client:load />

  <Fragment slot="about">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Chunk size is the most consequential knob in most retrieval systems and the one most often set
      by copying a tutorial. The reason is that its effects are invisible. You pick 1000 and 200,
      the pipeline runs, embeddings come back, and nothing tells you that a third of your chunks
      begin halfway through a sentence, or that the overlap you configured collapsed to zero because
      your paragraphs are longer than the step.
    </p>
    <p>
      By the time it shows up, it shows up as a recall number, and a recall number does not say
      which boundary was wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>The approach</h2>
    <p>
      Seven strategies, implemented over source offsets rather than detached strings. That is the
      whole trick: because every chunk knows where it came from, the cuts can be painted <em
        >in place</em
      > on your own document, and the regions two chunks both claim can be shaded differently from the
      regions only one does.
    </p>
    <p>
      The implementations follow the shape of the ones people actually use: LangChain's character,
      recursive-character and Markdown-header splitters, plus the sentence-window pattern from
      LlamaIndex. The recursive splitter in particular descends the separator ladder and then <em
        >merges the pieces back up</em
      > to the size budget, which is the step naive re-implementations skip and the step that decides
      what the chunks look like. Without it a document of short lines becomes a document of short chunks.
    </p>
    <p>The measurements are chosen to catch the failures that hide:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Overlap achieved versus requested.</strong> Requested overlap is a ceiling, not a promise.
        A boundary-respecting splitter can only step back to a boundary that exists, so a large overlap
        against large natural units silently becomes none.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sentence cuts.</strong> How many chunks begin or end mid-sentence. It is the single most
        common reason a retrieved chunk is unusable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Index expansion.</strong> Characters stored divided by characters of source. Overlap is
        not free: every duplicated token is embedded once and paid for again on every retrieval that returns
        both neighbours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Size distribution.</strong> A tight cluster under the budget means the strategy is working.
        A bimodal one means your document has two kinds of section in it and one strategy will not serve
        both.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where it stops being trustworthy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The semantic strategy is not embedding-based. It measures topic shift with cosine similarity
        over sentence term vectors, which needs no network call and finds coarse boundaries well,
        but misses paraphrase: two sentences saying the same thing in different words score as
        unrelated. It is labelled "lexical" for that reason.
      </li>
      <li>
        Token counts come from the same estimator as the token counter, and carry the same 10 to 15%
        error. Token budgets are converted to character budgets using the ratio measured on <em
          >your</em
        >
        text, which is more honest than assuming four, and still an approximation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Sentence detection is rule-based. It knows about common abbreviations and single initials,
        and it will still be wrong on text with unreliable punctuation: transcripts, OCR output,
        anything without terminal periods.
      </li>
      <li>
        This shows you where a splitter cuts. It does not tell you whether retrieval improves. Only
        an evaluation over your own queries does that, and no visualiser substitutes for it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
